Description: Software update page in the settings menu, with live output
 Updating the unit meant an ssh session and a shell, which is exactly the
 thing this build set out to remove: the XDJ400 has a touchscreen, no
 keyboard and no desktop behind Mixxx. The settings menu gains a SOFTWARE
 UPDATE entry that opens a page running the on-Pi updater and showing its
 terminal output live, line by line, as it installs.
 .
 New widget WUpdatePanel (src/widget/wupdatepanel.{h,cpp}, skin tag
 <UpdatePanel>) owns the status line and the transcript. It runs
 "sudo -n /usr/local/bin/update-pioneered.sh --no-reboot": the path is fixed
 in C++ rather than taken from the skin because this runs as root and a skin
 is a folder of XML anyone can drop in; -n because there is no terminal
 behind the window to answer a password prompt, so a missing sudoers rule has
 to fail immediately with a message rather than hang forever. Output from
 both streams is merged so the log reads exactly as it would in a terminal,
 carriage returns are turned into newlines so apt's progress lines stack
 instead of overwriting, ANSI escapes are stripped, and the block count is
 capped so a long run cannot grow without bound on a 1 GB Pi. The log is
 dragged with a finger (QScroller): there is no wheel, and the scrollbar is
 far too thin to hit.
 .
 UPDATE is tap-again-to-confirm on the same 5 s disarm timer as POWER OFF --
 it installs packages and wants a restart, so one stray tap mid-set must not
 start it. While it runs, UPDATE and BACK are withdrawn: apt is mid-way
 through dpkg and there is nothing safe to press. Closing the page (or the
 menu) deliberately leaves a running update running, and the widget's
 destructor detaches from the process rather than killing it, because a
 half-finished dpkg is far worse than a lost window. When it finishes
 cleanly, RESTART NOW appears; rebooting reuses the settings menu's ladder
 (logind, then passwordless sudo).
 .
 The plumbing mirrors the Wi-Fi page exactly: LibraryControl owns the
 [Library],update_* controls and forwards them to the panel bound through
 Library::bindUpdatePanel, and the skin reads update_page / update_running /
 update_arm / update_done for its layout. One new control, menu_subpage, is
 1 while any sub-page is up: a skin Connection drives a property from a
 single control, so "menu hidden while Wi-Fi OR update is open" needs a
 control of its own rather than two bindings fighting over visibility.
